  "query_preview": "..\nThe combination of ample moisture and an upper-\nlevel disturbance passing across portions of Southeast Arizona \nfrom the east today will result in widespread showers and \nthunderstorms...especially south and east of Tucson. These \nstorms will be capable of producing heavy rainfall, with \nlocalized rainfall amounts between 1 and 3 inches. Given how \nsaturated soils are across the area from storms over the past \nweek, there is an increased potential that rainfall runoff to \nproduce flash flooding. A Flash Flood Watch remains in effect \nfor portions of Southeast Arizona (including Graham, Greenlee, \nCochise and Santa Cruz counties) through 10 PM MST this evening. \n\nThe upper pattern this morning continues to be defined by that\nslow moving upper-low (seen by its distinct swirl in GOES WV\nimagery) in west central New Mexico and an anomalously strong \nridge of high pressure centered over northwest Kansas. The upper\npattern remains in flux today as the upper-low moves over Graham\nand Greenlee counties by 21/18Z today as the upper high center\nmoves SE into central Oklahoma. Given slower trajectory of the\nupper-low, modest lift/deformation aloft will remain present \nacross the eastern CWA today when deep moisture remains. This\nwill result in another day of widespread showers and thunderstorms\nwith the potential for heavy rain to the east and south of\nTucson. This is supported once again by the 21/00Z HREF with a\n10 percent probability of storms producing in excess of 3 inches\nof rainfall in the Flash Flood Watch areas between 21/18 and \n22/03Z. These areas got hit pretty hard with heavy rain \nyesterday, which should increase the potential that any \nadditional heavy rain today will result in flash flooding. \n\nDespite the fact that we will still have daily afternoon and\nevening showers and thunderstorms the second half of the work\nweek, there will be a distinct downturn in activity. The upper-\nhigh center will move from Oklahoma today to southern New Mexico\n24/00Z Thursday afternoon then up to the Four Corner by 26/00Z \nSaturday afternoon. The approaching upper-high will increase \nafternoon temperatures once again to around 5 to 8 degrees above\nnormal. The combination of drier air and modest mid-level flow \nwill increase the potential for showers and thunderstorms next \nweekend to be more of the severe variety. In other words, the \nMonsoon rolls-on.",
